# Tater Talk Content & Messaging Guidelines

## Brand Voice
- **Conversational and approachable**: "Just talk" instead of "Initialize voice input"
- **Accessibility-focused**: Emphasize ease of use and inclusivity
- **Technology-simple**: Avoid technical jargon, focus on benefits

## Key Messaging Themes
1. **Ease of Use**: "No downloads, no setup", "Just open and talk"
2. **Universal Access**: "Works on all devices", "Cross-platform"
3. **Real-time**: "Instant", "Live", "As you speak"
4. **Hands-free**: "Talk to your computer", "Voice control"

## Headline Patterns
- **Main tagline**: "The easiest way to talk to your computer!"
- **Section headers**: Use "Build", "See", "Try", "Start" action words
- **Feature benefits**: Focus on user outcomes, not technical specs
- **CTA buttons**: "Start Talking Now", "Try Free", "Watch Demo"

## Audio/Speech Terminology
- Prefer "speech-to-text" over "voice recognition"
- Use "talk" instead of "speak" when possible
- "Voice commands" rather than "vocal instructions"
- "Real-time transcription" for technical accuracy

## Accessibility Language
- "Perfect for users with mobility challenges"
- "Hands-free computing"
- "Inclusive design"
- Avoid terms like "disabled" - focus on capabilities

## Social Proof Elements
- Accuracy percentages: "99.5% accuracy"
- Speed metrics: "50ms response time"
- Availability: "24/7 accessible"
- Trust indicators: "Privacy focused", "No data stored"